---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Digital Photo Frame">
  <section class="prose max-w-none">

    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">
      Digital Photo Frame
    </h1>

    <!-- Project Description -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Project Description
    </h2>

    <p>
      This project is a wireless digital photo frame built around an ESP32 and
      touchscreen display. The goal was to create a standalone device that allows
      photos to be added wirelessly without requiring a wired connection, removable
      storage, or a dedicated mobile application.
    </p>

    <p>
      The photo frame hosts its own web interface, allowing a user to connect with
      a phone or computer, scan a QR code displayed on the frame, and upload images
      directly through a web browser. Uploaded images are stored on an SD card and
      displayed as part of an automatically cycling slideshow.
    </p>

    <p>
      In addition to the firmware, I designed a custom PCB containing the ESP32,
      USB programming interface, battery charging and protection circuitry, power
      regulation, and connections for the display module. The project combines
      embedded programming, web development, digital hardware design, power
      electronics, and user-interface development in a single device.
    </p>


    <!-- How it Works -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      How it Works
    </h2>

    <p>
      The ESP32 controls the display, touchscreen, SD card, and wireless
      communication. During normal operation, JPEG images stored on the SD card
      are decoded and displayed sequentially on the screen to create a slideshow.
      The firmware keeps track of the available image files and periodically
      advances to the next image.
    </p>

    <p>
      Photos can also be added wirelessly. The ESP32 hosts a local web server that
      provides an upload interface which can be accessed from a phone or computer.
      A QR code displayed by the photo frame directs the user to the upload page,
      eliminating the need to manually enter an address.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before an image is uploaded, the browser interface allows the user to crop,
      rotate, and position the photo. The image is then resized to match the
      display resolution and converted to JPEG format before being transferred to
      the ESP32. Performing this processing in the browser reduces the amount of
      image processing required by the microcontroller.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once received, the ESP32 stores the image on the SD card, where it becomes
      available to the slideshow. The web interface can also display the files
      currently stored on the frame and provides controls for removing images.
    </p>


    <!-- Schematic and PCB -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Schematic and PCB
    </h2>

    <p>
      A custom PCB was designed to integrate the major electrical subsystems of
      the photo frame into a single board. The design includes an ESP32-WROOM
      module, USB-C power and data connections, a USB-to-UART programming
      interface, battery management circuitry, voltage regulation, and the
      connections required by the touchscreen display module.
    </p>

    <p>
      The power system is designed around a single-cell LiPo battery. Separate
      regulation stages provide the supply rails required by the display and
      digital electronics, while the USB-C connection provides both external
      power and a programming interface for the ESP32.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/PhotoFrame.png"
        alt="Block diagram of the analog synthesizer"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/photoPCB.png"
        alt="Block diagram of the analog synthesizer"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>


    <!-- Firmware -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Firmware and Software
    </h2>

    <p>
      The firmware is divided into several modules so that image handling,
      networking, user-interface control, and hardware interaction can be
      developed independently.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">
      Image Storage and Slideshow
    </h3>

    <p>
      At startup, the ESP32 scans the SD card for JPEG images and builds a list
      of available files. The images are decoded directly from the SD card and
      sent to the TFT display. A slideshow manager cycles through the stored
      images at a defined interval while allowing the touchscreen interface to
      temporarily interrupt normal playback.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">
      Wireless Photo Upload
    </h3>

    <p>
      A web server running directly on the ESP32 provides the browser interface
      used to manage the photo library. HTTP routes handle the main webpage,
      supporting files, image uploads, file listings, and photo deletion.
      Because the interface is served by the photo frame itself, no dedicated
      application is required on the user's phone or computer.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">
      Browser-Side Image Processing
    </h3>

    <p>
      The upload page performs image preparation before transferring a photo to
      the frame. The user can select portrait or landscape orientation, crop the
      image, rotate it, and adjust its position. The browser then generates an
      appropriately sized JPEG that can be stored and displayed directly by the
      embedded system.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">
      QR Code Interface
    </h3>

    <p>
      The firmware can generate a QR code containing the address of the photo
      upload page and render it directly on the TFT display. This provides a
      simple connection workflow: the user opens the upload screen on the photo
      frame, scans the displayed code, and is taken directly to the browser
      interface.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">
      Touchscreen and User Interface
    </h3>

    <p>
      Touch input is handled separately from the slideshow and is routed through
      a UI state system. This allows the display to switch between normal photo
      playback and interactive screens such as menus, wireless configuration,
      photo uploading, and device settings. The interface is structured so that
      additional configuration options can be added without modifying the core
      slideshow logic.
    </p>


    <!-- Next Steps -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Next Steps
    </h2>

    <p>
      The next stage of the project is hardware bring-up and debugging of the
      custom PCB. The individual electrical subsystems will be tested to verify
      the power rails, USB programming interface, battery operation, ESP32
      functionality, and communication with the display before the complete
      system is assembled.
    </p>

    <p>
      The firmware will also continue to be refined. Planned improvements include
      a more polished touchscreen interface, better photo-library management,
      improved image handling and slideshow behavior, and additional user
      settings.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once the electronics and firmware are finalized, the last major step will
      be designing and 3D printing a physical enclosure that houses the display,
      PCB, battery, and supporting hardware as a finished standalone photo frame.
    </p>


    <style>
      .project-figure {
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
      }

      .project-image {
        width: 100%;
        display: block;
        border-radius: 8px;
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
        background: #f5f5f5;
      }

      .project-figure figcaption {
        margin-top: 0.5rem;
        text-align: center;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        opacity: 0.7;
      }

      .image-placeholder {
        width: 100%;
        min-height: 320px;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        border: 1px dashed #aaa;
        border-radius: 8px;
        background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08);
        font-size: 1rem;
        opacity: 0.7;
      }
    </style>

  </section>
</BaseLayout>